PLANT OPS DIGEST - EXTRA
Friday, March 13, 2026

A short one between issues. We spent two days at the Pacific Plant Automation Expo in San Jose. Five notes.

1. Every booth said "AI." About one in six could show a deployment at a plant with under 500 people and a named reference willing to talk. Ask for the reference before the demo, not after.

2. The autonomous forklift vendors have won the floor. Four companies, all shipping, all quoting under $120,000 per unit with a two-year payback against a $24-an-hour operator. The humanoid demos drew crowds and zero purchase orders that we could find.

3. The MES vendors are scared of the scheduling startups and are pricing accordingly. Two of the big ones announced "lite" scheduling modules bundled free with the MES license. Whether free software that nobody configures beats paid software that someone does is the question of the next two years.

4. Vision inspection is a commodity now. Six vendors, roughly the same accuracy, roughly the same price. The differentiation has moved to how fast you can add a new SKU without a vendor visit. One vendor claimed four hours; a plant manager in the audience said "prove it" and they did, on stage, with a bag of pretzels.

5. The most-attended session was not about AI at all. It was a panel of three plant managers on how they got their maintenance teams to actually use a CMMS. The answer, in all three cases, was a tablet at the machine and a manager who checked the log every morning.

Full notes and photos on the site. The regular issue lands Wednesday.

